# The Colorado Department of Public Health and Environment (CDPHE) publishes its disease data on
# its own ArcGIS Server. The two fields below call it over SOAP, with requests written by hand
# against the Esri schema that the server's WSDL declares.

extend type Query {
	"""
	Animals in Colorado that tested positive in 2017 for rabies, plague, tularemia or West Nile virus, from the
	Colorado Department of Public Health and Environment (CDPHE), one page at a time, the earliest collected first.
	It answers questions about animal diseases that can pass to pets and people, such as which cats caught plague,
	where dogs had rabies, and where rabid bats and skunks were found. The 341 records are 7 cats (6 with plague and
	1 with tularemia), 2 dogs with rabies, 63 rabid bats, 93 rabid skunks, other wildlife, birds, horses and 126
	pools of mosquitoes with West Nile virus. The state publishes this map for 2017 only. For the yearly number of
	animals with rabies, plague or tularemia per county up to 2025, use cdpheAnimalDiseaseCases.
	"""
	cdpheAnimalsTestedPositive(
		"Only animals that tested positive for this disease. Leave it out for every disease."
		disease: CdpheZoonoticDisease
		"Only animals of this group, for example DOMESTIC_ANIMAL for the cats and dogs. Leave it out for every group."
		specimenType: CdpheSpecimenType
		"""
		Text that the species contains, in upper or lower case, for example cat, skunk or bat. The text can match part
		of a name, so dog also finds prairie dogs: pass specimenType DOMESTIC_ANIMAL for pets. Leave it out for every
		species.
		"""
		species: String = ""
		"The Colorado county the animal was found in, for example Boulder or La Plata, in upper or lower case. Leave it out for every county."
		county: String = ""
		"The page to return, starting at 1. A value below 1 returns the first page."
		page: Int! = 1
		"The number of animals on a page, from 1 to 50. A value outside that range is moved to the nearest limit."
		size: Int! = 10
	): CdpheAnimalTestedPositivePage!

	"""
	The number of animals in Colorado that tested positive for rabies, plague or tularemia in each year from 2016 to
	2025, per county, for the whole state, or per month, from the reportable disease dataset of the Colorado
	Department of Public Health and Environment (CDPHE). It answers questions such as how many rabid animals were
	found in Denver last year, whether plague turned up in a county where an owner lives, or in which months rabies
	peaks. In 2025, 69 animals tested positive for rabies across Colorado, 5 of them in Denver County.
	The counts come sorted by year, newest first, then by disease, then by count, highest first, or by month in
	calendar order. The dataset has a row for every county, year and disease, zeros included, so pass onlyWithCases
	to keep the rows that have cases, and narrow the years to keep the answer small.
	"""
	cdpheAnimalDiseaseCases(
		"Only this disease. Leave it out for rabies, plague and tularemia."
		disease: CdpheAnimalDisease
		"Whether to count per county, for the whole state, or per month of the year."
		groupedBy: CdpheCaseGrouping! = COUNTY
		"""
		The Colorado county, for example Denver, Jefferson or El Paso, in upper or lower case. It applies to counts
		grouped by COUNTY. Leave it out for every county.
		"""
		county: String = ""
		"The first year to count, for example 2023. The dataset starts in 2016. Leave it out to start there."
		fromYear: Int
		"The last year to count, for example 2025, the latest year in the dataset. Leave it out to end there."
		toYear: Int
		"Keep only the rows with at least one animal that tested positive."
		onlyWithCases: Boolean! = false
		"The page to return, starting at 1. A value below 1 returns the first page."
		page: Int! = 1
		"The number of counts on a page, from 1 to 50. A value outside that range is moved to the nearest limit."
		size: Int! = 10
	): CdpheAnimalDiseaseCountPage!
}

"One page of animals in Colorado that tested positive for a disease, from CDPHE."
type CdpheAnimalTestedPositivePage {
	"The animals on this page."
	animals: [CdpheAnimalTestedPositive!]!
	"The page that was returned, starting at 1."
	page: Int!
	"The number of pages available for the same filters and size."
	totalPages: Int!
	"The number of animals that match the filters, counted across every page."
	totalAnimals: Int!
}

"""
An animal, or a pool of mosquitoes caught in a trap, that tested positive in Colorado in 2017 for a disease that
animals can pass to pets and people.
"""
type CdpheAnimalTestedPositive {
	"The identifier of the record in the state's map."
	id: ID!
	"The species as the state recorded it, for example Cat, Skunk or Big brown bat, Eptesicus fuscus."
	species: String
	"The group the animal belongs to, such as a domestic animal or wildlife."
	specimenType: CdpheSpecimenType
	"The disease the animal tested positive for."
	disease: CdpheZoonoticDisease
	"The Colorado county the animal was found in, for example Larimer."
	county: String
	"The day the sample was collected. The state left it out for 165 of the 341 records, among them every rabid animal."
	collectedDate: Date
	"The ZIP code where the animal was found, for example 80524. The state left it out for 18 records."
	zipCode: String
	"""
	The latitude where the animal was found, rounded to two decimals of a degree, about one kilometre, because the
	point can be the home of a pet.
	"""
	latitude: Float
	"The longitude where the animal was found, rounded to two decimals of a degree, about one kilometre."
	longitude: Float
}

"The diseases on CDPHE's map of the animals that tested positive in Colorado in 2017."
enum CdpheZoonoticDisease {
	"Rabies, found in bats, skunks, foxes, a raccoon, a coyote and two dogs."
	RABIES
	"Plague, found in cats, prairie dogs, foxes and a flea."
	PLAGUE
	"Tularemia, also called rabbit fever, found in rabbits, a fox and a cat."
	TULAREMIA
	"West Nile virus, found in pools of mosquitoes, birds and horses."
	WEST_NILE_VIRUS
}

"The groups of animals on CDPHE's map of the animals that tested positive."
enum CdpheSpecimenType {
	"Pets: the cats and dogs."
	DOMESTIC_ANIMAL
	"Wild mammals other than bats and rodents: skunks, foxes, rabbits, a raccoon and a coyote."
	WILDLIFE
	"Bats of several species, such as the big brown bat."
	BAT
	"Birds such as hawks, owls, crows and a bald eagle."
	BIRD
	"Horses."
	HORSE
	"Rodents such as prairie dogs."
	RODENT
	"Pools of mosquitoes caught in traps."
	MOSQUITO_POOL
	"Fleas."
	FLEA
}

"One page of yearly counts of animals that tested positive in Colorado, from CDPHE."
type CdpheAnimalDiseaseCountPage {
	"The counts on this page."
	counts: [CdpheAnimalDiseaseCount!]!
	"The page that was returned, starting at 1."
	page: Int!
	"The number of pages available for the same filters and size."
	totalPages: Int!
	"The number of counts that match the filters, counted across every page."
	totalCounts: Int!
}

"""
The number of animals that tested positive for one disease in Colorado in one year, in one county, in the whole
state, or in one month, from CDPHE's reportable disease dataset.
"""
type CdpheAnimalDiseaseCount {
	"The disease."
	disease: CdpheAnimalDisease
	"The year, from 2016 to 2025."
	year: Int!
	"What the count covers: a county, the whole state, or a month."
	groupedBy: CdpheCaseGrouping
	"The county, for example Denver, for a count grouped by COUNTY. Unknown holds the animals without a county."
	county: String
	"The month, for example August, for a count grouped by ONSET_MONTH."
	month: String
	"The number of animals that tested positive. Null when the state suppressed the count or left it out."
	total: Int
	"Whether the state suppressed the count, which the dataset shows as **."
	suppressed: Boolean!
	"The number of people living in the county that year, for a count grouped by COUNTY."
	population: Int
	"""
	The animals that tested positive for every 100,000 people living in the county, rounded to two decimals. The
	state divides the animal count by the human population, so Denver's 5 rabid animals in 2025 give 0.68.
	"""
	ratePer100kPeople: Float
}

"The animal diseases in CDPHE's reportable disease dataset."
enum CdpheAnimalDisease {
	"Animals with rabies. On the 2017 map, these were mostly bats and skunks."
	RABIES
	"Animals with plague. On the 2017 map, these were cats, prairie dogs and foxes."
	PLAGUE
	"Animals with tularemia, also called rabbit fever. On the 2017 map, these were rabbits, a fox and a cat."
	TULAREMIA
}

"What a count in CDPHE's reportable disease dataset covers."
enum CdpheCaseGrouping {
	"One count for each Colorado county, and Unknown for the animals without a county."
	COUNTY
	"One count for the whole of Colorado."
	STATE
	"One count for each month of the year."
	ONSET_MONTH
}
